This is the most frequently asked LLD problem (LeetCode 146). Both `get` and `put` must be [[g:O(1)]].

**Why two structures are required:** a `HashMap` provides O(1) lookup but no order; a list provides order but O(n) lookup. Combine them so the map points [[o:directly to a list node]], making lookup, removal, and insertion all O(1).

It must be a [[g:doubly]] linked list because removing a node requires its predecessor. Two sentinel nodes (`head`, `tail`) eliminate every special null case.

<pre><code><span class="k">class</span> LRUCache {
    <span class="k">private final</span> <span class="k">int</span> cap;
    <span class="k">private final</span> Map&lt;Integer, Node&gt; map = <span class="k">new</span> HashMap&lt;&gt;();
    <span class="k">private final</span> Node head = <span class="k">new</span> Node(<span class="n">0</span>, <span class="n">0</span>), tail = <span class="k">new</span> Node(<span class="n">0</span>, <span class="n">0</span>);
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="f">LRUCache</span>(<span class="k">int</span> cap) {
        <span class="k">this</span>.cap = cap;
        head.next = tail; tail.prev = head;      <span class="c">// two sentinel nodes</span>
    }
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="k">public</span> <span class="k">int</span> <span class="f">get</span>(<span class="k">int</span> key) {
        Node n = map.<span class="f">get</span>(key);
        <span class="k">if</span> (n == <span class="k">null</span>) <span class="k">return</span> -<span class="n">1</span>;
        <span class="f">moveToFront</span>(n);                          <span class="c">// just used =&gt; most recent</span>
        <span class="k">return</span> n.val;
    }
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="k">public</span> <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">put</span>(<span class="k">int</span> key, <span class="k">int</span> val) {
        Node n = map.<span class="f">get</span>(key);
        <span class="k">if</span> (n != <span class="k">null</span>) { n.val = val; <span class="f">moveToFront</span>(n); <span class="k">return</span>; }
        <span class="k">if</span> (map.<span class="f">size</span>() == cap) {                  <span class="c">// full =&gt; evict the last node</span>
            Node lru = tail.prev;
            <span class="f">remove</span>(lru); map.<span class="f">remove</span>(lru.key);
        }
        Node fresh = <span class="k">new</span> Node(key, val);
        map.<span class="f">put</span>(key, fresh); <span class="f">addFirst</span>(fresh);
    }
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="k">private</span> <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">moveToFront</span>(Node n) { <span class="f">remove</span>(n); <span class="f">addFirst</span>(n); }
    <span class="k">private</span> <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">remove</span>(Node n) { n.prev.next = n.next; n.next.prev = n.prev; }
    <span class="k">private</span> <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">addFirst</span>(Node n) {
        n.next = head.next; n.prev = head;
        head.next.prev = n; head.next = n;
    }
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="k">private static class</span> Node {
        <span class="k">int</span> key, val; Node prev, next;
        <span class="f">Node</span>(<span class="k">int</span> k, <span class="k">int</span> v) { key = k; val = v; }
    }
}</code></pre>

Each node must also store its `key`: during eviction you have the node but need the `key` to remove its map entry.

:::deep
- `LinkedHashMap` implements this in five lines with `accessOrder=true` and an overridden `removeEldestEntry`. [[o:Mentioning it is useful]] because it demonstrates library knowledge, but the interviewer almost always wants the manual implementation.
- a common extension is **thread safety**. One global lock is simple but blocks all operations; real implementations use [[g:sharding]] such as Caffeine or Guava, with multiple segments and one lock per segment.
- LRU is [[r:not always the best policy]]: one full-table scan can evict all hot data. [[o:LFU]] or [[o:W-TinyLFU]] (Caffeine) resists scans at the cost of frequency-tracking memory.
- adding TTL requires a third structure, such as an expiry min-heap or timing wheel; see 22.8
- in practice, Redis uses [[o:approximate]] LRU by sampling several random keys and evicting the oldest, avoiding a linked list for millions of keys. Mentioning this earns points.
- useful test cases include capacity = 1, overwriting an existing key, getting a missing key, and verifying eviction order after interleaved get/put operations
:::

Topic 10 discusses the algorithms at system level; here you must [[o:write the code]].

**Token bucket** — it permits bounded bursts, which is why it is the most widely used approach:

<pre><code><span class="k">class</span> TokenBucket {
    <span class="k">private final</span> <span class="k">long</span> capacity;
    <span class="k">private final</span> <span class="k">double</span> refillPerMs;
    <span class="k">private</span> <span class="k">double</span> tokens;
    <span class="k">private</span> <span class="k">long</span> lastRefill;
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="f">TokenBucket</span>(<span class="k">long</span> capacity, <span class="k">double</span> refillPerSec) {
        <span class="k">this</span>.capacity = capacity;
        <span class="k">this</span>.refillPerMs = refillPerSec / <span class="n">1000.0</span>;
        <span class="k">this</span>.tokens = capacity;                   <span class="c">// full at initialization</span>
        <span class="k">this</span>.lastRefill = System.<span class="f">currentTimeMillis</span>();
    }
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="k">synchronized</span> <span class="k">boolean</span> <span class="f">tryAcquire</span>(<span class="k">int</span> n) {
        <span class="f">refill</span>();
        <span class="k">if</span> (tokens &lt; n) <span class="k">return</span> <span class="k">false</span>;
        tokens -= n;
        <span class="k">return</span> <span class="k">true</span>;
    }
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="k">private</span> <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">refill</span>() {
        <span class="k">long</span> now = System.<span class="f">currentTimeMillis</span>();
        tokens = Math.<span class="f">min</span>(capacity, tokens + (now - lastRefill) * refillPerMs);
        lastRefill = now;                          <span class="c">// clamp at capacity: idle time cannot accumulate without bound</span>
    }
}</code></pre>

Use [[o:lazy refill]], calculated when called, rather than a periodic refill thread. It requires no dedicated refill thread and remains accurate to the millisecond.

**Sliding window log** — exact, at the cost of O(requests in the window) memory:

<pre><code><span class="k">boolean</span> <span class="f">allow</span>(String key, <span class="k">int</span> limit, <span class="k">long</span> windowMs) {
    <span class="k">long</span> now = System.<span class="f">currentTimeMillis</span>();
    Deque&lt;Long&gt; q = log.<span class="f">computeIfAbsent</span>(key, k -&gt; <span class="k">new</span> ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;());
    <span class="k">synchronized</span> (q) {
        <span class="k">while</span> (!q.<span class="f">isEmpty</span>() &amp;&amp; q.<span class="f">peekFirst</span>() &lt;= now - windowMs) q.<span class="f">pollFirst</span>();
        <span class="k">if</span> (q.<span class="f">size</span>() &gt;= limit) <span class="k">return</span> <span class="k">false</span>;
        q.<span class="f">addLast</span>(now);
        <span class="k">return</span> <span class="k">true</span>;
    }
}</code></pre>

<table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Memory</th><th>Allows bursts</th><th>Accuracy</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Token bucket</td><td>O(1)</td><td>yes, up to capacity</td><td>high</td></tr>
<tr><td>Fixed window</td><td>O(1)</td><td>yes, up to 2× at the boundary</td><td><b>low</b> — boundary error</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sliding window log</td><td>O(n)</td><td>no</td><td>exact</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sliding window counter</td><td>O(1)</td><td>limited</td><td>good approximation</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

:::deep
- [[r:fixed-window boundary error]]: with a limit of 100 per minute, 100 requests at 10:00:59 and another 100 at 10:01:00 produce 200 requests in one second while remaining "within the rules". Interviewers frequently probe this case.
- in a distributed system, state must live in [[g:Redis]], and the read-modify-write operation must be atomic through a Lua script or `INCR` plus `EXPIRE`. Per-instance in-memory limits become N times the intended limit across N instances.
- a Redis token bucket stores `tokens` and `lastRefill` in a hash and wraps refill plus deduction in one Lua script
- return the correct [[o:429]] status with `Retry-After` and `X-RateLimit-Limit/Remaining/Reset` headers (17-rest-api-design.contracts-errors-lifecycle.q2)
- choose the key by layer: API key for partners, user for authenticated consumers, and IP for anonymous users. [[r:IP-only limiting is wrong]] when customers behind NAT or CGNAT share an address.
- distinguish rate limiting, which prevents abuse, from [[o:load shedding]], which protects against overload using current load, and from concurrency limits. Real systems commonly need all three.
- the hard testing problem is freezing time. Inject `Clock` or `Supplier&lt;Long&gt;` instead of calling `System.currentTimeMillis()` directly; this makes the code testable and demonstrates good design.
:::

These are two classic problems. Evaluation is not about one "correct answer" but about [[o:responsibility boundaries]] and the ability to add new types.

**Parking lot — a compact model:**

<pre><code><span class="k">enum</span> SpotType { MOTORBIKE, COMPACT, LARGE }
<span class="c">//</span>
<span class="k">interface</span> Vehicle { SpotType <span class="f">requiredSpot</span>(); String <span class="f">plate</span>(); }
<span class="c">//</span>
<span class="k">interface</span> PricingStrategy { Money <span class="f">price</span>(Ticket t, Instant out); }
<span class="k">interface</span> SpotAllocator { Optional&lt;Spot&gt; <span class="f">allocate</span>(Vehicle v); }
<span class="c">//</span>
<span class="k">class</span> ParkingLot {
    <span class="k">private final</span> SpotAllocator allocator;      <span class="c">// change the allocation policy</span>
    <span class="k">private final</span> PricingStrategy pricing;      <span class="c">// change the pricing policy</span>
    <span class="k">public</span> Ticket <span class="f">park</span>(Vehicle v) { ... }
    <span class="k">public</span> Money <span class="f">unpark</span>(String ticketId) { ... }
}</code></pre>

Those two `interface` declarations answer the extension question *"What if pricing changes during peak hours?"*: add another `PricingStrategy` and [[g:do not modify `ParkingLot`]].

**Elevator — the key distinction is separating *commands* from *scheduling*:**

<pre><code><span class="k">enum</span> Direction { UP, DOWN, IDLE }
<span class="c">//</span>
<span class="k">class</span> Request { <span class="k">int</span> floor; Direction dir; }        <span class="c">// hall call</span>
<span class="k">class</span> Elevator {                                <span class="c">// only knows how to move ITSELF</span>
    <span class="k">int</span> current; Direction dir;
    TreeSet&lt;Integer&gt; stops;
    <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">step</span>() { ... }                          <span class="c">// move one floor</span>
}
<span class="k">interface</span> Scheduler {                           <span class="c">// choose which car serves the request</span>
    Elevator <span class="f">assign</span>(Request r, List&lt;Elevator&gt; cabins);
}</code></pre>

An `Elevator` must not know about other cars. If it does, it violates single responsibility and cannot be tested independently.

:::deep
- the [[r:number-one mistake]] is putting everything in a 300-line `ParkingLot` class with `if (type == ...)` scattered throughout. An extension question exposes it immediately.
- [[o:state the invariants aloud]]: one vehicle occupies one spot, one spot holds one vehicle, and a paid ticket cannot be paid again. Then show where the code enforces them.
- allocate efficiently with `Map&lt;SpotType, PriorityQueue&lt;Spot&gt;&gt;` in O(log n) instead of scanning every spot. Complexity analysis earns points even in an LLD round.
- for elevators, use the [[g:SCAN/elevator]] algorithm, traveling fully in one direction before reversing. Disk I/O scheduling uses the same idea, and connecting it to operating systems is valuable.
- a `TreeSet` for `stops` turns "the next stop in the current direction" into O(log n) `ceiling()`/`floor()` calls, cleaner than manually sorting
- concurrency matters when many people press buttons simultaneously (22-low-level-design-ood.patterns-in-interview-code.q3). At minimum say, *"I will use a request queue and one dispatcher thread."*
- the same template solves vending machines (State pattern), cinema reservations (seat locking), chat, and libraries. Mastering the template matters more than memorizing each problem.
:::

This natural extension of LRU is a miniature Redis, making it a favorite follow-up question.

**Requirements:** `put(k, v, ttl)`, `get(k)`, automatic expiry, and no memory leaks.

**Three expiry approaches — production systems use all three:**

<table class="cmp"><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Mechanism</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Lazy</td><td>check expiry during <code>get</code></td><td>cheap, but an unread key can <b>remain forever</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>Active sampling</td><td>a periodic job samples random keys and deletes expired ones</td><td>steady low CPU cost; cleans cold keys</td></tr>
<tr><td>Expiry min-heap</td><td>a job reads exactly the next key due to expire</td><td>precise, but the heap needs cleanup when keys are overwritten</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<pre><code><span class="k">class</span> TtlStore {
    <span class="k">private final</span> Map&lt;String, Entry&gt; map = <span class="k">new</span> ConcurrentHashMap&lt;&gt;();
    <span class="k">private record</span> Entry(String val, <span class="k">long</span> expireAt) {
        <span class="k">boolean</span> <span class="f">alive</span>(<span class="k">long</span> now) { <span class="k">return</span> expireAt == <span class="n">0</span> || now &lt; expireAt; }
    }
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="k">public</span> <span class="k">void</span> <span class="f">put</span>(String k, String v, <span class="k">long</span> ttlMs) {
        <span class="k">long</span> exp = ttlMs &lt;= <span class="n">0</span> ? <span class="n">0</span> : System.<span class="f">currentTimeMillis</span>() + ttlMs;
        map.<span class="f">put</span>(k, <span class="k">new</span> Entry(v, exp));
    }
<span class="c">    </span>
    <span class="k">public</span> Optional&lt;String&gt; <span class="f">get</span>(String k) {
        <span class="k">long</span> now = System.<span class="f">currentTimeMillis</span>();
        Entry e = map.<span class="f">get</span>(k);
        <span class="k">if</span> (e == <span class="k">null</span>) <span class="k">return</span> Optional.<span class="f">empty</span>();
        <span class="k">if</span> (!e.<span class="f">alive</span>(now)) {
            map.<span class="f">remove</span>(k, e);                 <span class="c">// remove(k,v): do not delete a newer entry</span>
            <span class="k">return</span> Optional.<span class="f">empty</span>();
        }
        <span class="k">return</span> Optional.<span class="f">of</span>(e.<span class="f">val</span>());
    }
}</code></pre>

The two-argument `map.remove(k, e)` is [[o:important]]: another thread may `put` a new value between the read and delete. Removing by key alone would [[r:delete newly written data]].

:::deep
- Redis uses exactly lazy expiry plus active sampling: every 100 ms it samples 20 keys with TTLs, deletes expired keys, and repeats if more than 25% were expired. [[g:Mentioning this is a major plus.]]
- a [[o:timing wheel]], such as Netty's `HashedWheelTimer` or Kafka's implementation, outperforms a min-heap for millions of expirations with O(1) insertion/removal instead of O(log n)
- combining TTL with LRU requires both a capacity limit and expiry. Caffeine already provides `expireAfterWrite` plus `maximumSize`; mention it to demonstrate library awareness.
- `System.currentTimeMillis()` [[r:can move backwards]] when NTP adjusts the clock, so use `System.nanoTime()` for elapsed durations. This small detail is distinctly senior.
- persistence requires a WAL or snapshots, such as Redis AOF and RDB. It is usually outside a 45-minute scope but worth identifying.
- a common extension is `getOrLoad` using cache-aside plus [[o:cache-stampede]] protection through `computeIfAbsent` or single-flight, directly connecting to the Microservices caching chapter
:::

Testability is an architectural property. Keep domain decisions in small objects or pure functions; put clocks, randomness, ID generation, persistence, messaging, and scheduling behind narrow injected ports. This lets tests control inputs without exposing private fields or adding production-only switches.

<pre><code><span class="k">interface</span> Clock { Instant <span class="f">now</span>(); }
<span class="k">interface</span> IdSource { String <span class="f">next</span>(); }
<span class="k">final class</span> HoldService {
    <span class="k">private final</span> Clock clock;
    <span class="k">private final</span> HoldRepository holds;
    <span class="c">// constructor injection; no Instant.now() hidden in domain logic</span>
}</code></pre>

Express each aggregate invariant and legal transition close to the state it protects: a hold cannot expire before creation, a released hold cannot be confirmed, balance-changing commands must preserve the ledger equation. Return explicit outcomes instead of using exceptions for every expected rejection. Make side effects occur after a decision through ports, and define idempotency where commands can be retried.

Use a layered test portfolio:

- [[b:Example/unit tests]] cover boundaries and state transitions with a fake clock and deterministic IDs.
- [[b:Property/model-based tests]] generate command sequences and assert invariants after every step; a simple reference model can catch optimized-implementation mistakes.
- [[b:Contract/integration tests]] verify adapters against the real database, broker, serializer, or HTTP contract.
- [[b:Concurrency tests]] first identify the linearization point, then coordinate targeted interleavings with barriers/latches and bounded timeouts. Add sustained stress or OpenJDK jcstress for Java Memory Model questions; passing one stress run is evidence, not a proof.

:::deep
Do not use `Thread.sleep` as synchronization. Inject virtual time for TTL and refill logic; use explicit signals to know when a worker reached the intended point. Record seeds and shrink failing property cases. Assert final state and externally visible history, not implementation timing.

Thread safety is part of the public contract: document which methods are safe concurrently, compound-operation semantics, callback/reentrancy rules, fairness, blocking, cancellation, and ownership of returned mutable data. Prefer designs with a single owner or immutable snapshots when they make illegal interleavings unrepresentable. The concurrency reasoning framework is covered in (16-project-concurrency-whiteboard.technical-storytelling-fundamentals.q3).
:::
